{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1501930000,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDExOTQxNjIwODc="},"added":["conference agenda item"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1501930000,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDExOTQxNjIwODg="},"added":["documentation"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1501930001,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDExOTQxNjIwODk="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1502302159,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyMTMzNjQzNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1689#issuecomment-321336434"},"message":"Echoing brief discussion in the hangout:\n\n- yes, having clearly defined topics would be useful\n\n- no, I think that \"a single monolithic tutorial piece would not work\" is incorrect.  We could have a possibly redundant, but not as complete as specific topics, introduction to the main principles to datalad/git-annex, demonstrating the full blown workflow.  And then compliment it with points to the specific topics \"to learn more\".  I will try to sketch one up today/tomorrow.\n\n- above allocation to roles is imho superficial. e.g. why \"basic VC for large files\" would not be relevant for G, D, and M?  depending on the coverage detail it might even be useful to A. Pretty much the same could be asked about any other topic.  So it is not really a role, but rather scope and target usecase which would identify who should read what.   That is why IMHO having an overall overview would be great to point to the capabilities of the datalad/git-annex which could be explained in greater detail in specific topics\n\n- Python interface.   Should also be at least noted in the \"introductory tutorial\", since that came as a surprise to some audience and they were quite thrilled to see it happen in Python ;-)  Eventually we could make all code/script blocks be in both shell or Python based on the choice of the audience ;)  that would be neat imho. So may be it is worthwhile thinking about creating materials with BOTH annotated somehow so we could switch easily","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1502444581,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyMTc3MDYwMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1689#issuecomment-321770602"},"message":"Re monolithic tutorial piece:\nAt the end, I agree with @yarikoptic , that we should aim for a single \"entry point\", linking more specific docs. But regarding \"How do we get there?\", @mih might be right: Let's build those specific (standalone) docs/demos/examples with a certain audience in mind first and only then the more general ones which link to them.\n\nRe roles:\nAllocation might not be ideal yet, but I think it's a good approach to write the pieces with those roles in mind. Not each topic has to be divided that way, though. (D) doesn't tell anything about the existing knowledge on git-annex, of course. But topics marked for (D) should not be relevant for others (contributing, how to build plugins/extensions, ...)\n\nRe python interface:\nWould be cool to always have both and let the docs render depending on user's choice (while testing always discovers both). But I'd start by writing everything based on CLI. Let's add python when we have a reasonable state of documentation.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1503992605,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyNTU4MjAxOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1689#issuecomment-325582019"},"message":"OK, opinions are clear, new website in-place and for further work are being made -- closing this one ...","files":null}]}